---
import BaseLayout from '../components/BaseLayout.astro';
import AdSensePlaceholder from '../components/AdSensePlaceholder.astro';
import MBTITestIsland from '../components/MBTITestIsland';

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "Is this test the official MBTI?",
    answer: "No. TypeInsight is an independent personality assessment inspired by Jungian typology and the Myers-Briggs framework. The official MBTI® is a registered instrument by The Myers-Briggs Company. Our test is for educational and entertainment purposes only.",
  },
  {
    question: "How accurate is this test?",
    answer: "Research on MBTI-style instruments shows test-retest reliability ranging from 0.61 to 0.87 over short periods. Type assignments can shift over time and context. Use your result as one lens, not a fixed label.",
  },
  {
    question: "How long does the test take?",
    answer: "Most people complete all 10 questions in under three minutes. There is no time pressure — answer thoughtfully.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need to create an account?",
    answer: "No account, no email, no sign-up. The test runs entirely in your browser and we do not store your answers.",
  },
  {
    question: "What do I do after I get my result?",
    answer: "Read your full type profile to understand your cognitive style, strengths, and growth edges. If you want to explore your type further with a professional, our result page links to therapist-matching services.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Free MBTI Personality Test — 10 Questions | TypeInsight"
  description="Take our free, research-based MBTI-style personality test. 10 questions, instant results, no sign-up required."
  faqItems={faqItems}
>
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 pt-14 pb-20">
    <div class="mb-12">
      <p class="mono-cap mb-4">Free Test</p>
      <h1 class="font-sans font-extrabold text-4xl md:text-5xl text-ink mb-4"
          style="letter-spacing:-0.05em;">
        Discover Your Personality Type
      </h1>
      <p class="text-ink-mute font-sans text-base max-w-xl leading-relaxed">
        Answer honestly — there are no right or wrong answers. Choose the option that
        feels most natural, not the one you think you should choose.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AdSensePlaceholder format="horizontal" />

    <div class="py-8">
      <MBTITestIsland client:load />
    </div>

    <AdSensePlaceholder format="rectangle" />

    <!-- Science article -->
    <article class="max-w-2xl mt-16 pt-12 font-sans text-ink">
      <h2 class="font-sans font-extrabold text-3xl mb-6" style="letter-spacing:-0.04em;">The Science Behind Personality Types</h2>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-extrabold text-xl mb-3 mt-8" style="letter-spacing:-0.03em;">Carl Jung and the Origins of Type Theory</h3>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The idea that human beings can be grouped into distinct personality categories stretches
        back to ancient Greece, but the modern framework descends directly from the Swiss
        psychiatrist Carl Gustav Jung. In his 1921 work <em>Psychological Types</em>, Jung
        proposed that people differ fundamentally in how they direct mental energy (introversion
        versus extraversion) and how they perceive the world and make decisions. These preferences,
        he argued, were not deficits or pathologies but stable orientations that shaped behaviour
        across the lifespan.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Jung identified two primary <em>attitudes</em> — Introversion (I) and Extraversion (E) —
        and four <em>functions</em>: Sensing, Intuition, Thinking, and Feeling. The dominant
        function, combined with the attitudinal orientation, produces eight basic psychological
        types in Jung's original model.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-extrabold text-xl mb-3 mt-8" style="letter-spacing:-0.03em;">From Jung to Myers-Briggs</h3>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        During World War II, Katharine Cook Briggs and her daughter Isabel Briggs Myers set out
        to make Jung's abstract theory practically useful. They believed that if people understood
        their own type — and the types of those around them — they could find work better suited
        to their strengths and collaborate more effectively. Over two decades of iterative
        development, they constructed a self-report questionnaire that eventually became the
        Myers-Briggs Type Indicator® (MBTI®), first published in 1962.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Myers and Briggs added a fourth dichotomy not explicit in Jung's original writing:
        Judging (J) versus Perceiving (P), describing how a person orients themselves to the
        outer world — preferring closure and structure, or openness and flexibility. Combined
        with the other three dimensions, this produces 16 distinct four-letter type codes still
        used widely today.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-extrabold text-xl mb-3 mt-8" style="letter-spacing:-0.03em;">The Four Dimensions Explained</h3>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong class="text-ink">Extraversion (E) vs. Introversion (I)</strong> — This
        dimension concerns the direction of energy flow. Extraverts are energised by social
        interaction and external stimulation; they think out loud and prefer breadth of connection.
        Introverts restore energy through solitude and inward reflection; they favour depth over
        breadth and often process extensively before speaking.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong class="text-ink">Sensing (S) vs. Intuition (N)</strong> — This dimension
        describes how people take in information. Sensing types trust direct, concrete experience.
        They are detail-oriented, practical, and grounded in the present. Intuitive types
        gravitate toward patterns, possibilities, and the big picture. They are comfortable with
        abstraction and often project forward into potential futures.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong class="text-ink">Thinking (T) vs. Feeling (F)</strong> — This dimension
        concerns decision-making. Thinking types prioritise logical consistency and objective
        criteria; they are comfortable delivering difficult feedback if the facts support it.
        Feeling types weigh interpersonal impact and personal values; they seek harmony and
        consider how decisions affect the people involved.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <strong class="text-ink">Judging (J) vs. Perceiving (P)</strong> — This dimension
        describes how people relate to structure. Judging types prefer closure: they like plans,
        schedules, and decisions made. Perceiving types prefer to keep options open: they adapt
        fluidly and often do their best work when allowed to improvise.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-extrabold text-xl mb-3 mt-8" style="letter-spacing:-0.03em;">Reliability, Validity, and Critique</h3>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The MBTI has been the subject of substantial empirical scrutiny. Test-retest reliability
        studies — where the same person takes the test twice within a few weeks — show correlations
        in the range of 0.61 to 0.87 depending on the dimension. A notable finding from longitudinal
        research is that roughly 35–50% of respondents receive a different four-letter type when
        retested after five weeks, most often flipping on a single dimension close to the midpoint.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Critics argue that the forced binary format (you must choose E or I, not a continuous
        score) artificially polarises people who fall near the midpoint of any dimension. The
        Big Five personality model — which measures Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion,
        Agreeableness, and Neuroticism on continuous scales — is generally regarded as having
        stronger predictive validity for outcomes like job performance and academic achievement.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Despite these limitations, the MBTI remains one of the most widely used personality
        frameworks in organisational development, career counselling, and team building, with
        millions of assessments administered each year. Used thoughtfully — as a starting point
        for self-reflection rather than a fixed label — personality typing can offer genuine
        insight.
      </p>

      <h3 class="font-sans font-extrabold text-xl mb-3 mt-8" style="letter-spacing:-0.03em;">Practical Applications</h3>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Where personality typing delivers the most practical value is in raising self-awareness.
        Understanding that your discomfort in open-plan offices may reflect genuine introversion —
        not antisocial behaviour — can help you design better working conditions. Recognising
        that a colleague's need for structured agendas reflects a Judging preference — not
        rigidity — can reduce interpersonal friction.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-mute leading-relaxed">
        Career research on MBTI type suggests meaningful correlations between type and occupational
        preferences. Thinking-Judging types frequently gravitate toward roles in engineering,
        law, and management. Feeling-Perceiving types often find fulfilment in counselling,
        arts, and education. Intuitive types tend to be overrepresented in academic and creative
        fields. These are tendencies, not destinies — but they are useful anchors when navigating
        career decisions.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- FAQ accordion -->
    <section class="max-w-2xl mt-14 pt-12">
      <h2 class="font-sans font-extrabold text-2xl text-ink mb-8" style="letter-spacing:-0.04em;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        {faqItems.map((item, i) => (
          <details open={i === 0} class="rounded-[28px] bg-paper px-6 py-5" style="border:1px solid var(--ink-line);">
            <summary class="font-sans font-semibold text-ink cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center text-sm">
              {item.question}
              <span class="text-ink-mute ml-4">&#9650;</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-ink-mute font-sans text-sm leading-relaxed">
              {item.answer}
            </p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
      <style>details[open] { border-color: #C2855E !important; }</style>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
